{"uuid": "4e041b25-b626-50d1-8901-2fa0e690aecc", "title": "nine midterm races that will decide control us senate 2026 09 07", "author": "", "url": "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/nine-midterm-races-that-will-decide-control-us-senate-2026-09-07/", "posted_url": "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/nine-midterm-races-that-will-decide-control-us-senate-2026-09-07/", "is_linking_to_external_website": false, "timestamp": "2026-09-07T12:05:43.867000+02:00", "source": "https://www.reuters.com", "crawled_at": "2026-09-07T14:28:47.217068+02:00", "text": "WASHINGTON, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Democrats need to pick up four seats from Republicans to win control of the 100-seat Senate in the midterm elections on November 3. While voters will pick candidates in 35 states, independent analysts only see nine of those races as competitive.\nDemocrats have a good chance to pick up a seat in North Carolina, where incumbent Republican Senator Thom Tillis is retiring.\nOpinion polls show former Democratic Governor Roy Cooper holding a steady lead over Republican operative Michael Whatley, an \u200bally of Republican President Donald Trump who most recently chaired the Republican National Committee.\nCooper is well known to North Carolina voters, having won four races as state attorney general and two more as governor in \u200c2016 and 2020, even as Trump carried the state in those years.\nWhatley also has decades of experience in politics but has not previously run for office, and his close ties to Trump could work against him at a time when the president's approval rating stands at 33%, the lowest of his political career, according to Reuters/Ipsos polling.\nDemocrats are playing defense in Michigan, where they hope to retain the seat held by retiring Senator Gary Peters. A loss in this closely contested state would complicate their efforts to win control of the chamber.\nDemocratic nominee Abdul El-Sayed, a progressive, faces lingering skepticism as he \u200btries to consolidate support after narrowly defeating centrist Representative Haley Stevens in a bitter primary. The former health official's criticism of Israel and association with left-wing streamer Hasan Piker galvanized the party's left flank but alienated many of \u200bthe state's Black and Jewish voters.\nHe faces Republican Mike Rogers, a former House member who lost a Senate race in 2024.\nPolls show a close race, and nonpartisan analysts rate it a \u2060toss-up, saying El-Sayed's left-wing candidacy could give Rogers a greater opportunity to win independent voters.\nThis Republican-dominated state has emerged as a surprise battleground this year. Polls show a close race between Democrat James Talarico, a state representative and former seminary student, and \u200bRepublican Ken Paxton, the state's attorney general.\nDespite allegations of marital infidelity that nearly forced him from office in 2023, Paxton unseated incumbent Senator John Cornyn with Trump's backing in a divisive primary. Since then, he has struggled to raise money -- forcing his party to \u200bspend money shoring up what would normally be considered a safe seat.\nTalarico has sought to win over independents and disaffected Republicans, but his past comments about gender identity could be a liability. Still, analysts see this race as a toss-up.\nIncumbent Republican Senator Dan Sullivan faces two liabilities in his reelection campaign: a credible challenge from a Democrat and another person on the ballot with the same name.\nDemocrat Mary Peltola previously held the state's sole House seat for a single term and has campaigned as a centrist. She garnered 82,000 votes to Sullivan's 69,000 in the state's nonpartisan primary in August, \u200bin what could be an ominous sign for the incumbent.\nSullivan also will have to contend with another Republican, retired teacher Dan J. Sullivan, who got enough votes to advance to the November election despite Republican attempts to knock him off the ballot.\nPeltola could \u200balso benefit from the state's ranked-choice voting system, which takes voters' second choices into account.\nPolling has been sparse in this race, but analysts see it as a toss-up.\nAfter a decade of Republican dominance, Iowa is home to competitive races this year, as the farm state struggles with \u200cTrump's tariffs and \u2060high fuel prices.\nRepresentative Ashley Hinson aims to keep the seat in Republican hands following the retirement of incumbent Senator Joni Ernst.\nShe faces state representative Josh Turek, a former Paralympic basketball player who has emphasized his bipartisan work in the state legislature.\nPolls show a close race, and analysts say it should be one of the most competitive contests this fall.\nDemocrats scrambled in July to find a replacement for Graham Platner, the progressive oyster farmer and political newcomer who dropped out after facing accusations of sexual assault, which he denied. The party quickly coalesced around Troy Jackson, a former lumberjack and state legislator who shares Platner's progressive politics.\nJackson, 58, takes on incumbent Republican Senator Susan Collins, a moderate who occasionally breaks with her party. First elected in 1996, Collins has won reelection easily even \u200bas the northeastern state has consistently backed Democratic presidential candidates.\nRecent \u200bpolls have shown Jackson holding a three- to four-percentage-point \u2060lead.\nDemocratic Senator Jon Ossoff is running for reelection against Republican Representative Mike Collins, a conservative backed by Trump.\nGeorgia has emerged as a top battleground state in recent elections. Trump narrowly won the state in the 2024 presidential election, while Ossoff, 39, narrowly won his seat in a 2021 runoff. Analysts see him as a possible 2028 presidential candidate.\nCollins has cultivated an edgy social-media persona that \u200bhas occasionally backfired, as in 2024, when he faced criticism for suggesting Democratic President Joe Biden directed an assassination attempt on Trump. In recent weeks, he has been under scrutiny \u200bfor racist posts by a former \u2060aide, which his campaign has disavowed.\nMost polls have shown Ossoff leading Collins, and nonpartisan analysts say he holds the advantage in this race.\nFormer Democratic Senator Sherrod Brown is hoping voters in Ohio remember his long record of economic populism as he tries to win back his old job.\nFirst elected in 2006, Brown was defeated by Republican Bernie Moreno in 2024, a year when Trump won the once-competitive state by 11 percentage points.\nHe is now trying to unseat incumbent Republican Senator Jon Husted, who was appointed to the seat in 2025 after \u2060then-Senator JD Vance resigned \u200bto serve as Trump's vice president. Husted previously served as lieutenant governor and secretary of state.\nOhio has trended steadily Republican over the past decade, but \u200brecent polls show Brown leading Husted by three to four percentage points and analysts view the race as a toss-up.\nThe winner of this race will serve the remainder of Vance's term, through January 2029.\nNew Hampshire holds its primary election on September 8 in the contest to fill retiring Democratic Senator Jeanne Shaheen's \u200bseat. Republicans are expected to nominate John Sununu, who previously served in the Senate from 2003 to 2009, while Democratic Representative Chris Pappas is leading the Democratic field. Nonpartisan analysts say Democrats are favored in this contest."}
