assination attempt on Trump. In recent weeks, he has been under scrutiny \u200bfor racist posts by a former \u2060aide, which his campaign has disavowed.\nMost polls have shown Ossoff leading Collins, and nonpartisan analysts say he holds the advantage in this race.\nFormer Democratic Senator Sherrod Brown is hoping voters in Ohio remember his long record of economic populism as he tries to win back his old job.\nFirst elected in 2006, Brown was defeated by Republican Bernie Moreno in 2024, a year when Trump won the once-competitive state by 11 percentage points.\nHe is now trying to unseat incumbent Republican Senator Jon Husted, who was appointed to the seat in 2025 after \u2060then-Senator JD Vance resigned \u200bto serve as Trump's vice president. Husted previously served as lieutenant governor and secretary of state.\nOhio has trended steadily Republican over the past decade, but \u200brecent polls show Brown leading Husted by three to four percentage points and analysts view the race as a toss-up.\nThe winner of this race will serve the remainder of Vance's term, through January 2029.\nNew Hampshire holds its primary election on September 8 in the contest to fill retiring Democratic Senator Jeanne Shaheen's \u200bseat. Republicans are expected to nominate John Sununu, who previously served in the Senate from 2003 to 2009, while Democratic Representative Chris Pappas is leading the Democratic field. Nonpartisan analysts say Democrats are favored in this contest."}
